Hello list,

Being the colleague in question, I'd thought I'd share my reflections first.

On lines 157-226 in the script, Pernelle's data are analysed using 
random forests. The idea here is to see if the response pattern varies 
at all between the different conditions and the different types of 
speakers. If it does, then a classifier ought to reduce the 
classification error rate from the baseline (= most frequent category). 
With 38 outcome categories, the error rate is still large (84%, down 
from 88%), and I'm not too sure a whole lot can be learnt from it.

On lines 228-404, I tried a different tack - by reinterpreting the 
question somewhat. The idea here is to *assess the degree to which 
different participants agree with each other and whether this degree of 
agreement varies between conditions and types of speakers*.

Consider the following simplified example:

| SPEAKER | CONDITION | LABEL |
| ID1           | C1                 | A         |
| ID2           | C1                 | A         |
| ID3           | C2                 | A         |
| ID4           | C2                 | B         |

ID1 and ID2 agree with each other on the label choice, as do ID1 and 
ID3, as do ID2 and 3. ID4 disagrees with everyone. Restructuring this 
information:

| SPEAKER PAIR | CONDITION PAIR | AGREEMENT |
| ID1-ID2            | C1-C1                    | 1                     |
| ID1-ID3            | C1-C2                    | 1                     |
| ID1-ID4            | C1-C2                    | 0                     |
| ID2-ID3            | C1-C2                    | 1                     |
| ID2-ID4            | C1-C2                    | 0                     |
| ID3-ID4            | C2-C2                    | 0                     |

 From this, we can derive the following:

- Agreement within C1: 100%
- Agreement within C2: 0%
- Agreement between C1 and C2: 50%

Scale this up to 1,000 speakers, 38 labels, and a couple more conditions 
and you've got Pernelle's data.

My thinking was as follows. We can restructure the data as above and 
then analyse the degree of agreement in a statistical model (I used OLS 
regression for speed) to assess how it varies between combinations of 
conditions and types of speakers. However, the confidence intervals from 
the regression model wouldn't be any good as the model ignores the 
dependencies in the data. Specifically: once we know that (a) ID1 and 
ID2 agree, that (b) ID1 and ID3 agree as well, we know also know that 
(c) ID2 and ID3 agree. This is why in the script (lines 345-388) I 
compute confidence intervals for the degree of agreement using the 
bootstrap.

Anyway, I'm not 100% if all, or indeed any, of this makes sense, so I, 
too, would welcome your feedback.

Cheers,
Jan

